course: dictionary
lesson: 8009
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which one you need. The word reed is a good example. «Scribes pressed a cut reed into wet clay, making wedge-shaped marks.» You can cut it and press it into clay, so here a reed is a plant.
explain: «The Mole hears only the wind in the reeds, but he rows on where the Rat nods, up a backwater.» The boat is on water, and the reeds are the tall plants growing beside it. The clues rowing and backwater tell you where to look.
explain: A reed can also be a thin strip inside an instrument such as the clarinet or oboe. It shakes to make the sound. When you meet reed, ask which meaning fits the clues. Then try that meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Writers also use reed in a picture-word way: «a slender reed» means a thin, weak person or thing. The clue slender tells you this is not about a plant at all.
fact: A reed is a tall, thin, grass-like plant with a hollow stem.
fact: Reeds grow in or near water, in ponds, marshes and riverbanks.
fact: Reeds give homes to birds and insects.
fact: In music, a reed is a thin strip that shakes to make the sound, as in the clarinet and oboe.
fact: «A slender reed» is a literary way of describing a thin, weak person or thing.
exercise: «Scribes pressed a cut reed into wet clay.» In this sentence, what is a reed? | A small bird that sings in marshes | A thin strip that shakes to make a sound | A thin, weak person | *A tall, grass-like plant with a hollow stem
exercise: Which instrument uses a reed to make its sound? | *Clarinet | Drum | Trumpet | Flute
exercise: «A slender reed» describes a person. What does reed mean in this phrase? | A tall plant in a pond | *A thin, weak person or thing | A strip that shakes to make music | A riverbank
test: Where do reeds usually grow? | Inside a clock | On the moon | In dry sand | *In ponds, marshes and riverbanks
test: Why are reeds useful to birds and insects? | They are used to make the wind blow | They can fly | *They give them homes | They are made of metal
exam: «The Mole hears only the wind in the reeds, but he rows on.» What does reeds mean here? | *Tall, grass-like plants growing by the water | Thin, weak people | Pieces of wood in a boat | Musical strips inside an instrument
